---
title: Bill Pay — Case Study
stylesheets:
  - ../../css/tokens.css
  - ../../css/reset.css
  - ../../css/components/section.css
  - ../../css/components/hero.css
  - ../../css/components/header.css
  - ../../css/components/subheader.css
  - ../../css/components/body.css
  - ../../css/components/video.css
  - ../../css/components/image-group.css
  - ../../css/components/principles.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/scene-gallery-accordion/scene-gallery-accordion.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/scene-gallery-2/scene-gallery-2.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/scene-accordion/scene-accordion.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/walkthrough-gallery/gallery.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/walkthrough-gallery/visual.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/walkthrough-gallery/caption.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/walkthrough-gallery/progress-bar.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/walkthrough-gallery/legend.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/cockpit/steps-rail.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/cockpit/members-rail.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/cockpit/toggle.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/cockpit/tag.css
  - ../../css/widgets/bill-pay/cockpit/center-stage.css
scripts:
  - ../../js/bill-pay/walkthrough-gallery.js
  - ../../js/bill-pay/cockpit-widget.js
  - ../../js/bill-pay/scene-gallery-accordion.js
  - ../../js/bill-pay/scene-gallery-2.js
  - ../../js/bill-pay/scene-accordion.js
---
{% extends "base.njk" %}
{% from "hero.njk" import hero %}
{% from "section.njk" import section %}
{% from "section-header.njk" import sectionHeader %}
{% from "section-body.njk" import sectionBody %}
{% from "section-visual.njk" import sectionVisual %}
{% from "two-block-header.njk" import twoBlockHeader %}
{% from "gallery-navigation.njk" import galleryNavigation %}
{% from "scene-gallery-accordion.njk" import sceneGalleryAccordion %}
{% from "scene-gallery-2.njk" import sceneGallery2 %}
{% from "scene-accordion.njk" import sceneAccordion %}

{% block body %}
<main>
  {# 1. Hero #}
  {{ hero(
    "../../assets/illustrations/bill-pay/hero/bill-pay-hero-illustration.png",
    "Lorem ipsum is a dummy placeholder text <br /><br />It lets people view page layouts and fonts without getting ",
    "When simplicity is not the right design strategy",
    "Lorem ipsum is a dummy placeholder text used in design and publishing. It lets people view page layouts and fonts without being distracted by real words."
  ) }}

  {# 2. Section header with subheading, no visual #}
  {% call section() %}
  {{ sectionHeader("Money on the move", "Business background") }}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "QuickBooks is the backbone of financial operations for millions of US small businesses. <br>While it has always been the industry leader for accounting, for years, the actual money movement happened elsewhere. So when it came down to payments, users would open their bank portals, write checks, or rely on whatever third-party workarounds they could grab.",
    "This created a fragmented experience; customers struggled with sync-latency and data discrepancies that compromised their financial accuracy. QuickBooks identified an opportunity to close this gap by integrating bill payment capabilities directly into the platform, capturing the entire bill lifecycle within a single, seamless workflow."
  ]) }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# 3. Gallery section #}
  {% call section("section--grey-light") %}
  {{ sectionHeader("Click, Click, Done", "Exiting Experience (V1)") }}
  {# Gallery intro paragraph moved to statBlock above #}
  {% call sectionVisual(true) %}
  <div class="gallery">
    <iframe class="gallery__visual" id="gallery-visual" title="Walkthrough step"></iframe>
    {{ galleryNavigation("gallery") }}
  </div>
  {% endcall %}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "Launched in 2022 as a multi-step wizard, Bill Pay V1 was tailored to serve QuickBooks' core audience of small businesses, managing their own books, and processing up to 30 bills per month.",
    "The experience was rooted in a cognitive model of simplicity, and the overhead of moving money was wrapped inside a friendly, sequential wizard with a minimalist interface. Paying a bill to a vendor was easy like ordering pizza, so that even non-professional users could navigate their bill payments with confidence."
  ]) }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# 4. Tipping Point — one section, two visual blocks (white then green).
     White block: real bill-pay-stat image + its intro copy, appended via
     firstExtra right after the existing body paragraph.
     Green block: real team-members image, unchanged. #}
  {% set statBlock %}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "A year after launch, while Bill Pay was gaining traction with small businesses, data revealed a unique segment that promised to be a massive growth engine.<br>A specific group, representing less than a quarter of the QuickBooks user base, was responsible for over 90% of the total bill processing volume.",
    "It was a classic \"80/20 rule\" in its most painful version. The potential was obvious, but so was the problem, as this wasn't the customer Bill Pay had been built for."
  ]) }}
  <div class="section-image-group">
    <img class="section-image-group__image" src="../../assets/illustrations/bill-pay/bill-pay-stat.png" alt="" />
  </div>
  {% endset %}
  {% call twoBlockHeader(
    "Tipping Point",
    "Mission & Scope",
    [],
    [],
    "section--green-light",
    "",
    statBlock
  ) %}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "High-Value Billers were Mid-Market companies processing hundreds and thousands of bills monthly. They used QuickBooks for accounting and recording their bills, but were leaking out to execute payments.",
    "The team went on an early discovery, trying to map out the gaps.<br>They met with AP teams, talked about their bill-payment workflows, and reviewed the current solution. The interviewees looked at that beautiful, sequential, multi-step wizard and said, \"Guys, we love you, but we're not going to use your payment product.\""
  ]) }}
  <div class="section-image-group">
    <img class="section-image-group__image" src="../../assets/illustrations/bill-pay/team-members.png" alt="" />
  </div>
  {{ sectionBody([
    "The existing experience couldn't support their scale for two main reasons.<br>First, a linear flow was like asking them to fill an Olympic pool with a spoon, as these people don't need an interface that would hold their scared hand. Second, and most importantly, payment execution at scale meant a multi-seat operation, segregation of duty, and a chain of finance roles managing the process.",
    "So what would it take to scale a lightweight payment feature into a Mid-Market AP machine? The required capabilities were scattered across legacy domains, guarded by different teams and organizations, and none of them were built to work together. Building this right went far beyond Bill Pay.",
    "Anyone who's worked on enterprise products knows this is a challenging alignment puzzle, getting multiple teams, each with their own metrics and roadmaps, to move in the same direction at the same time.",
    "Leadership said go, and at that pivotal moment, I was brought in as Principal Designer.<br>My mission was to partner with design and cross-functional leads across QuickBooks to rethink our core capabilities, while building a new Bill Pay experience that could support payment execution at scale."
  ]) }}
  {{ sectionBody(["role:", "role:", "role:", "role:"]) }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# 5. Cockpit Widget section — header, body, widget, then a second trailing body #}
  {% call section() %}
  {{ sectionHeader("Stacked in one room", "Empathy & Ecosystem mapping") }}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "We started where the problem actually lived, understanding the insides of mid-market AP operations. Forty-plus interviews, operational workflows spread out in front of us, and what came back was a goldmine of pain points and gaps within their current toolsets and also ours.",
    "Getting cross-functional partners to feel the weight of that required more than a Slack summary. So we flew down to Mountain View, put everyone in the same room, and spent two days synthesizing our research findings, mapping the end-to-end AP lifecycle and the personas involved, and layered them against our existing experience."
  ]) }}
  {% call sectionVisual(true) %}
  <div class="cockpit-widget">
    <div class="cockpit-left-nav">
      <div class="cockpit-nav-panel">
        <div class="cockpit-headline-row">
          <h2 class="cockpit-headline">AP workflow, Journey & Empathy Mapping</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="cockpit-content-row">
          <div class="cockpit-steps-column-wrapper">
            <div class="cockpit-selected-bg" id="cockpit-selected-bg"></div>
            <div class="cockpit-connector-line"></div>
            <ol class="cockpit-steps-column" id="cockpit-steps-column"></ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cockpit-toggle-slot">
        <div class="cockpit-toggle" id="cockpit-toggle">
          <div class="cockpit-toggle__track journey" id="cockpit-toggle-track">
            <div class="cockpit-toggle__pill"></div>
            <p class="cockpit-toggle__label" id="cockpit-toggle-label-journey">Journey</p>
            <p class="cockpit-toggle__label" id="cockpit-toggle-label-empathy">Empathy</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cockpit-right" id="cockpit-right">
      <div class="cockpit-members-rail" id="cockpit-members-rail">
        <div class="cockpit-member-row" id="cockpit-member-row"></div>
        <div class="cockpit-highlight-track">
          <div class="cockpit-highlight-track__fill" id="cockpit-highlight-fill"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% endcall %}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "This high-level view was a breakthrough, as until that point, each team held a slice of it. It was the first time the whole flow came together as one linear map, which allowed us to move past siloed thinking and see clearly how a friction is traced back to a missing feature much earlier in the flow.<br>Vendor onboarding, mass bill ingestion, approvals, permissions - all of it was needed work, since QuickBooks had never operated inside that specific flow at this order of magnitude.",
    "Now that we had our scope and priorities set, our collaborative efforts branched, each with its own tasks. While partner teams focused on upstream challenges like managing roles and permissions, mass bill ingestion, and vendor onboarding, my mission was to reimagine the payment execution core and the cognitive architecture it required."
  ]) }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# 6. The AP manager #}
  {% call section("section--grey-light") %}
  {{ sectionHeader("The AP Manager", "Problem definition") }}
  {{ sectionBody([
    "The next thing was to deep dive into our primary user, the one who drives payment execution. On this seat, mindset is built around payment runs, while keeping a delicate balance between compliance, efficiency, and cash flow control.",
    "We also looked at how organizational dynamics define their role, and how their success is ultimately measured. For these AP managers, payment execution isn't a series of independent tasks. It's a high-stakes orchestration, a cohorted move, where every micro-adjustment ripples through the entire run."
  ]) }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# 7. Designing a cockpit — single white block (header + principles row). No
     second/continuation section — the gallery that used to live there is now
     its own section below (see "Volume_Productivity accordion"), so
     secondSection=false suppresses twoBlockHeader's second <section> entirely
     instead of leaving a real-but-empty green one behind. #}
  {% set principlesBlock %}
  <div class="principles-block">
    <div class="principles-column">
      <img class="principles-icon" src="../../assets/illustrations/bill-pay/volume-productivity.png" alt="" />
      <div class="principles-text">
        <div class="principles-tag"><p class="principles-tag__label"># Volume_Productivity</p></div>
        <p class="principles-caption">An orchestrated run, uninterrupted at every level</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="principles-divider"></div>
    <div class="principles-column">
      <img class="principles-icon" src="../../assets/illustrations/bill-pay/quality-of-decisions.png" alt="" />
      <div class="principles-text">
        <div class="principles-tag"><p class="principles-tag__label"># Quality_Of_Decisions</p></div>
        <p class="principles-caption">Cost, timing and risk, weighed before the run commits</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="principles-divider"></div>
    <div class="principles-column">
      <img class="principles-icon" src="../../assets/illustrations/bill-pay/Process-accountability.png" alt="" />
      <div class="principles-text">
        <div class="principles-tag"><p class="principles-tag__label"># Process_accountability</p></div>
        <p class="principles-caption">One authorization over the run, every bill traceable beneath it</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  {{ sectionBody([
    "In practice, instead of trying to simplify the data or hide the complexity, the design we were going for was about getting control over it while moving at speed. We traded our minimalist, empty spaces for a high-density view, surfacing all the context needed on every action right where it happened, visible in one view."
  ]) }}
  {% endset %}
  {{ twoBlockHeader(
    "Designing a cockpit",
    "Principles & Execution",
    [
      "The wizard paradigm was wrong not only because of the manual effort of handling one bill at a time, but because it stripped away the cross-parameter context, where every decision an AP manager makes depends on the bills sitting next to it.",
      "The goal was to allow the AP manager to follow a clear, defensible path from review to submission, to empower decision quality by providing a structured context, and to weigh every variable without losing momentum. The approach was anchored around three core principles:"
    ],
    [],
    "section--green-light",
    "",
    principlesBlock,
    secondSection=false
  ) }}

  {# Volume_Productivity accordion — replaces the chrome around Gallery 1
     (caption row, progress bar, 650px mock) with a vertical accordion
     rail; the mock itself (assets/scene-gallery/bill-pay/scenes.html) is
     unchanged. Own section directly below "Designing a cockpit" (badge
     06), unheadered so it doesn't consume a section-header badge number. #}
  {% call section("section--green-light") %}
  {{ sceneGalleryAccordion() }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# Accordion gallery — exploratory variant, placed above Gallery 2
     for direct visual comparison. Same content as Gallery 2, own white
     section, unheadered (so it doesn't consume a section-header badge
     number — Gallery 2's own numbering below is unaffected). Does not
     modify Gallery 2 in any way; may be deleted after comparison. #}
  {% call section() %}
  {{ sceneAccordion() }}
  {% endcall %}

  {# 8. Gallery 2 — own white section, not a continuation of section 7.
     No section header (undecided per build spec) — just the gallery. #}
  {% call section() %}
  {{ sceneGallery2() }}
  {% endcall %}
</main>
{% endblock %}
